## Your task
Identify which candidates are **real** chapter or section titles, and discard
false positives such as:
- ordinary sentences
- page numbers / running headers
- footnote references like "[12]"
- license / copyright boilerplate (e.g. Project Gutenberg headers, "START OF THE",
  "END OF THE PROJECT GUTENBERG EBOOK", "PLEASE READ THIS BEFORE YOU DISTRIBUTE")
- author bio, dedication, epigraph (UNLESS they have a real heading)
- entries inside an Index, Glossary, or Table-of-Contents listing
- the **word "Contents" / "Table of Contents" itself** when it heads such a
  listing (the listing duplicates the chapter structure and should NOT be a
  chapter of its own)
- short legal / metadata lines like "Title:", "Author:", "Release date:",
  "Language:", "Credits:"

## Output schema
Return strict JSON only — no prose, no markdown fences:

{
  "chapters": [
    {"anchor_id": "<echoed from input>",
     "title": "<cleaned-up title text in {{ source_language }}>",
     "level": <1 | 2 | 3>},
    ...
  ]
}

## Rules
- Preserve document order. Do NOT reorder.
- `level`: use **level 1 for the top-level chapters of this book** (whatever
  the author called them — "Chapter N", "I.", "Part II", "第三章", or just a
  numbered title). Only use level 2/3 for **sub-sections nested inside** a
  top-level chapter (e.g. "1.1 Background" under "1 Introduction", or
  "A. Petty-bourgeois Socialism" under "III. Socialist Literature"). When
  unsure, prefer level 1 — over-flattening is recoverable; over-nesting hides
  chapters under wrappers.
- `title`: clean up the candidate text:
    * combine two-line markers like "I." + "BOURGEOIS AND PROLETARIANS" into
      "I. Bourgeois and Proletarians";
    * normalize aggressive ALL-CAPS to title case unless the all-caps form is
      stylistic (e.g. "WAR" in a one-word title);
    * strip trailing page numbers, running heads, or stray punctuation.
- The cleaned `title` MUST stay in the source language ({{ source_language }});
  do NOT translate.
- Skip non-content sections (Index, Acknowledgments only if trivial, Copyright,
  Project Gutenberg boilerplate, etc.).
- If a candidate's `next_preview` shows it's actually a sentence continuation
  (the preview text would not logically follow a chapter break), drop it.
- If unsure, prefer INCLUDING the candidate at level 2 — better to
  over-segment than to lose chapter boundaries.

